  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Some areas of frost this morning again tonight.\nOtherwise, generally tranquil weather is expected to prevail \nthrough the middle portion of next week.\n\nHigh pressure will remain in place within the vicinity of our area \nthrough the middle of next week with continuing mild and dry \nconditions. \n\nA Frost Advisory remains in effect through 8 AM for portions of\nSE PA (excluding Philly and Delco) into southern NJ where the \ngrowing season remains active. Lows in these areas will be \ngenerally in the mid 30s. North of these areas generally expect \nlows getting down into the low 30s while Delmarva and the urban \ncorridor from around Wilmington through Philly will generally \nsee lows in the upper 30s to around 40 degrees.\n\nFor the remainder of today, we'll have a bit more in the way of\nsome high cloudiness around, but the winds will remain light \nand temperatures will moderate a few degrees compared to\nSaturday. Afternoon highs are forecast to be mostly in the low\nto mid 60s.\n\nFor tonight, low temperatures will be similar to this morning's\nlows, except a few degrees higher along and north of I-78. The\ncurrent forecast is for low to mid 30s across interior PA and NJ\nareas, and upper 30s to mid 40s for the urban corridor,\nDelmarva, and coastal areas. So we anticipate some areas of \nfrost developing again tonight, mainly for our PA and NJ areas\noutside of the urban corridor and immediate coast. Another Frost\nAdvisory will be considered once the current one expires.\n\nScattered to broken high cloud cover will persist into next week \ndespite surface high pressure. Temperatures are expected to increase \nback to above normal values early next week, with highs in the mid \n60s to low 70s and milder overnights (no frost/freeze potential). \nDry weather is expected to persist through the day Wednesday,\nwhen highs will reach well above normal into the low to mid \n70s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain is expected to return late next week. \n\nThe stretch of dry weather is expected to come to an end late next \nweek. It appears increasingly likely that a period or periods of \nrainfall will move into the area as a storm system affects the \nregion Thursday through at least Friday. Early in the week, a cold \nfront will be moving across the central part of the country. The \nparent low moves across the Great Lakes and into Canada later in the \nweek, while a secondary low develops and moves into the southern \nstates by mid week. This low is expected to strengthen as it moves \ninto the Mid Atlantic and northeast states later in the week, \nbringing our area a likely window to end our dry streak.\n\nForecast details remain uncertain since this is still several\ndays away, but at this point the best chances for rain appear \nto be late day Thursday into the day Friday. At this time, this \nappears it will be a beneficial rainfall event with no \nsignificant weather hazards anticipated, but it will have to be\nwatched closely as there is potential for amounts to be on the \nheavier side depending on how much Atlantic moisture is pulled \ninto the system.",
